; synthetic polymer. the defining material of the modern world.
; made from petroleum-derived monomers via polymerisation.
; 
; two main types:
; thermoplastic: can be remelted and reshaped. most common.
; PE (polyethylene): bags, bottles. PP (polypropylene): food containers.
; PVC: pipes, insulation. PET: drink bottles, polyester fabric.
; PS (polystyrene): foam packaging. nylon (polyamide): rope, clothing.
; thermoset: cross-linked, cannot be remelted.
; epoxy, bakelite, polyurethane (rigid foam), vulcanised rubber.
; 
; properties (thermoplastic):
; solid at room temperature. softens above glass transition temperature Tg.
; lightweight (density 0.9-1.4 g/cm³ — floats in water except PET, PVC)
; electrical insulator. thermal insulator.
; variable elasticity: PE is flexible (e≈0.6), PS is brittle (e≈0.2).
; not biodegradable (most). UV degrades over years → microplastics.

